WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.318
Safe logging starts with data minimization.

2
00:00:02.318 --> 00:00:10.433
A log should contain the information needed for a defined operational purpose, not every value that happens to be in scope.

3
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:14.684
The easiest sensitive log to manage is the one never written.

4
00:00:14.684 --> 00:00:17.002
Secrets do not belong in logs.

5
00:00:17.002 --> 00:00:23.571
Passwords, API keys, tokens, session identifiers, private keys, authorization headers, signing material, recovery codes, and sensitive configuration

6
00:00:23.571 --> 00:00:27.822
values should be excluded or represented through carefully designed safe markers.

7
00:00:27.822 --> 00:00:30.913
Personal and regulated data require the same discipline.

8
00:00:30.913 --> 00:00:38.255
Names, addresses, account details, device identifiers, location information, health or financial data, and other regulated fields should be minimized

9
00:00:38.255 --> 00:00:39.414
according to policy.

10
00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:44.437
Even when one field looks harmless, logs can aggregate context across many events.

11
00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:51.006
Avoid logging full objects, raw request bodies, large records, memory dumps, or broad exception details without review.

12
00:00:51.006 --> 00:00:59.894
These shortcuts can turn diagnostic storage into a sensitive data store that is copied, indexed, retained, and viewed by more people than the

13
00:00:59.894 --> 00:01:00.666
original system.

14
00:01:00.666 --> 00:01:04.144
Redaction should happen before data reaches the log sink.

15
00:01:04.144 --> 00:01:11.486
Once sensitive data is written to a local file, queue, agent, collector, or search system, removal becomes much harder.

16
00:01:11.486 --> 00:01:16.123
Treat redaction as part of event construction, not a later cleanup wish.

17
00:01:16.123 --> 00:01:19.601
Internal paths and implementation details should also be limited.

18
00:01:19.601 --> 00:01:26.942
Maintainers need enough context to investigate, but logs should not reveal unnecessary local directory layouts, internal topology, or configuration

19
00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:30.034
details to audiences that do not need them.

20
00:01:30.034 --> 00:01:40.467
A useful review question is: if this log line were copied into a ticket, retained for a year, or viewed by another operations team, would the data

21
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:41.626
still be appropriate?

22
00:01:41.626 --> 00:01:44.717
If not, redesign the event before shipping it.

23
00:01:44.717 --> 00:01:49.741
For C++ code, be especially cautious with helper functions that stringify entire objects.

24
00:01:49.741 --> 00:01:55.537
A convenience operator or serializer may include fields the caller did not intend to expose.

25
00:01:55.537 --> 00:02:00.560
Safe logging should choose fields deliberately instead of relying on broad object formatting.
